 "notes": [
  "float32 forward error is about 1e-7 of the largest output, which is the accumulation order over dstate, not an approximation.",
  "float16 and bfloat16 round once on the store; the recurrence itself runs in float32, so the error there is the output rounding of the type.",
  "Every gradient has exactly one writer, so the backward is deterministic: dA, dD and dB/dC are folded across the rows of one threadgroup and then collapsed by a second kernel, in a fixed order, instead of being accumulated with device atomics. determinism/* repeats three complete runs and requires bit-identical gradients.",
  "The backward is two dispatches: the row kernel plus one reduce kernel whose flat grid collapses per-head dA/ddt_bias/dD, per-(head, dim) dD, per-(batch, group, dstate) dB/dC and a per-head dt in one launch. The five zero-filled buffers and two torch reductions it also removes were seven more launches per step, which is what a single synchronized call pays for. The atomics were not the dominant cost at these shapes: interleaved against the atomic version on the same tensors the backward is about 1.05x faster, and the second dispatch costs 15-20 us by itself.",
  "A row spans `lanes` threads, the largest power of two up to min(dstate, 32), and one threadgroup holds the largest power of two up to 8 rows that divides dim, so dstate=16 fills the threadgroup with two rows instead of leaving half of it idle.",
  "Lane l of a row owns the four consecutive elements at 4l, so each thread issues one 16-byte request and the row's 32 stores land in one 128-byte line.",
  "The forward columns time the inference path: with nothing requiring grad the public entry point calls the kernel without building an autograd node. The backward columns build the graph and go through autograd.Function, which is what training pays.",
  "loss_graph_amortized_ms is the (y * weight).sum() + (state * weight).sum() expression on its own: five aten kernels over the same tensors, which every backward measurement here includes and the torch-ops baseline pays as well. backward_net_of_loss_amortized_ms subtracts it.",
  "Backward always computes in float32 and casts each gradient back to its input dtype, so a float16 step gets an accumulated-in-float32 gradient.",
  "The torch-ops baseline materialises the (batch, heads, dim, dstate) decay and drive tensors in separate kernels; the fused path never does.",
  "out_state writes in place and must not require grad; the differentiable path is the default one, which returns a fresh state tensor.",
  "compile_ms is the first call of each dtype, shader compile included.",
  "A single synchronized step is dominated by dispatch and wait: dispatch_floor_amortized_ms is one aten kernel on one element, measured in the same run, and the forward's single-call column sits far above its amortized column because of it. The amortized columns are what a decode loop pays per layer."
 ],
 "operator": "mamba2_state_update",
 "reference": "mamba2_state_update_reference evaluated in float64 on the CPU from the inputs rounded to the dtype under test; the torch-ops baseline is that same composition on the MPS tensors",
 "timing_protocol": [
  "min and median of 50 synchronized iterations after 10 warmup; the fused kernel is interleaved with the equivalent torch composition on the same MPS tensors, and with the same composition on the CPU; speedup uses the minimum because the machine is shared with other work",
  "a single synchronized call carries a fixed dispatch and wait on this machine, which is why the *_amortized_* columns time 20 launches between synchronizations: that is the cost a decode loop sees per layer",
  "backward_only_* builds the graph once and times autograd.grad with retain_graph, so it is the backward pass alone and not forward plus backward",
  "state_copy_* is dst.copy_(src) of one state tensor: the streaming floor for the same bytes the forward moves, measured in the same run",
  "dispatch_floor_* is one aten kernel on a one-element tensor, the launch cost the machine charges while these numbers were taken"
 ],
